  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 309 AM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nNorthwesterly upper flow Sunday evening is forecast to rapidly decay \ninto zonal flow (west to east) by Monday morning. Ensembles show \nthis flow pattern lasting through Tuesday, with a few weak systems \nembedded in the flow. A shortwave trough looks to develop over the \nregion Monday, though 24 hr precipitation guidance from both the NBM \nand LREF are in support of no precipitation with this system. \nTuesday, a weak embedded ridge is favored to be in place. Overall, \nmild and dry conditions are expected Sunday night through Tuesday. \nHighs are forecast in the 50s, with a 30-40% chance of seeing 60s in \nsome areas, and lows in the 20s to mid 30s. \n\nEnsembles show a deeper trough across the west Wednesday morning, \nlifting northeastward through the remainder of the forecast period. \nA surface low is expected to be favorably located at the leading \nedge of this trough, and in the left exit region of a modest 60-75 \nkt 500-mb jet. Theta-e surfaces show that moisture convergence is \nlikely across the area as the surface low approaches, with a moist \nPacific airmass meeting moist gulf air. Precipitation is forecast \nwith this system as it comes through. Around 90% of LREF guidance \nsupports this low tracking to the south of the forecast region, \nsuggesting that precipitation along and behind a cold front is the \nlikely solution. NBM guidance suggests that portions of Eastern \nColorado and Southwest Nebraska have a 50% chance or greater of lows \nbelow freezing with this system, which would support a mixed \nprecipitation event. GFS and EC model soundings suggest that rain \nand snow are the most likely at this time. \n\nThere is a reasonable amount of uncertainty on the timing of this \nevent, as seen in the most recent deterministic guidance with the \nGFS progressing the system slower than the EC and CMC. Turning to \nensembles, the trough axis at 500-mb Wednesday morning is \nconsistently over Southern California. However, GEFS, EC, and LREF \nspread is highest on the leading edge of the feature, which would \nindicate a faster progression, similar to the 00Z deterministic EC \nand CMC models. Looking at LREF members, this would put \nprecipitation onset and frontal passage around Wednesday evening. \nHowever, if a scenario similar to the GFS occurs, frontal passage \nand precipitation onset may not be until as late as Thursday \nnight. Currently, around 75% of LREF members show high \ntemperatures below 50 F Thursday, which would suggest the faster\nsolution, with precipitation and cooler temperatures arriving \nWednesday evening, is favored. Cooler temperatures would be \nexpected to remain through the end of the period. As for high \ntemperatures Wednesday ahead of the surface low's arrival, \nanticipated warm advection alongside NBM and LREF probabilistic\nguidance suggests mid 50s to lower 60s is appropriate.",
